#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-scope — the ask ledger: every ask the owner makes is written down, and 'done' is measured against it.

The board tracks what the BOX decided to do. Nothing tracked what the OWNER asked for, so a compound ask
("do X, and also Y") was closed on X, and a standing rule said once in a thread was forgotten by the next
session. This is the missing half: one row per ask, opened when it is made, closed only against evidence.

Ledger: ~/.cc/scope/<repo>.json  (sidecar like the board — never in git, so no merge noise).
Every owner message the capture path saw is logged to ~/.cc/scope/seen.log whether it was captured or not:
the classifier below is a filter, not an oracle, and `cc-scope missed` is how a miss is found and added.

An ask moves  open → claimed → merged → delivered → verified,  or leaves the queue as  declined.

MERGED IS NOT DELIVERED. A merge puts code on the default branch; it does not put anything on the owner's
screen. The daemon still runs the old code until it is restarted, the file is not linked until install.sh
runs, the tab does not carry the block until it is rendered. Rows were closed on "merged as PR #N" and the
owner himself found the chart was not there — so a PR number is no longer evidence of delivery, and no
sentence is. A row names its OWN verification, `check`: a shell command whose EXIT CODE decides. `deliver`
RUNS it. Exit 0 and the row is delivered, because something observable was true at that moment. Anything
else and the row stays exactly where it was.

A ROW WITH NO CHECK IS NOT DONE, IT IS UNVERIFIED. That is the honest answer and it is the point: not every
ask has a mechanical check, and where one genuinely cannot exist the row stays on the queue and says so
(`unverified`) rather than quietly counting as done. A person may still close it with `verify --evidence`,
which is them putting their own name on it — but that is a person's word, in a terminal, and it is refused
outright while the row names a check that has not passed. Nothing automated can reach `verified`.

DECLINED IS A DECISION, NOT A DROP: a session may judge that something should not be built, and that is a
legitimate answer — but it must say why, in its own words, and the reason stays in the ledger next to the ask.
`--why` is therefore required. An omission leaves an open item; a decision leaves a reason; the two can never
be mistaken for each other, which is the whole point of writing the asks down.

A CHECK THAT CANNOT RUN IS NOT A CHECK THAT FAILED. Ask a69's check was a script in its track's state dir;
the track finished, the janitor took the dir, and for five days the row read "merged, NOT delivered — its
check has not passed" while nothing about the ask had changed and nobody had measured anything. Both states
are rc≠0 and they mean opposite things, so they are now told apart: rc 126/127 is "no answer", it exits 3,
it says so on every line you read, and it never overwrites a check that once passed. The other half is that
a check should outlive its track — `check-cmd --script` copies the script in beside the ledger, and a check
that runs a script out of a directory which dies with its track is refused.

KEPT BEFORE IT IS CLASSIFIED. The classifier below is a filter and it has been wrong: "Ok well do whatever
you need to speed up the whole landing and review process" (2026-09-08) was dropped whole because the
sentence opened with an acknowledgement, and the only copy left of it was a seen.log line clipped at 160
characters and waiting to be trimmed. So every turn whose PROVENANCE says the owner wrote it is written to
~/.cc/scope/turns.jsonl in full, with its source, BEFORE the classifier runs. One the classifier keeps is
disposed of on the spot; one it skips stays PENDING until a person or a session says what it was — `classify`
(run the classifier over it again), `promote` (open the ask from the kept text, nothing retyped), or `dismiss
--why`. Compaction moves disposed records to turns-archive.jsonl and never touches a pending one: nothing
leaves that file for being old. Machine turns are excluded by provenance, not by wording, and are not kept at
all — see NOT_A_PERSON. What IS kept has its tokens redacted first (cc-slack's pattern) and one absurd
paste cut at TURN_MAX; `missed` prints the size of the record, because nothing ages out of it.

  cc-scope add <repo> "<ask>" [--src REF] [--track T] [--done-when "..."] [--check "<cmd>"] [--who W] [--at ISO]
  cc-scope list [<repo>] [--all] [--status S] [--track T] [--json]   unsettled asks (all repos if none named)
  cc-scope show <repo> <id>                                          one ask in full: parts, repeats, notes
  cc-scope claim <repo> <id> [--track T]                             a session/track has taken it on
  cc-scope check-cmd <repo> <id> "<command>" | --script <file>       the row's own verification; exit 0 = true
  cc-scope merged <repo> <id> --evidence "<PR or commit>"            it landed. NOT delivered — still work
  cc-scope deliver <repo> <id>                                       RUNS the check; exit 0 → delivered. A MERGED row whose
                                                                     check fails is written to the failures ledger, once per
                                                                     ask and merge (delivery/merged-not-delivered, via cc-green red)
  cc-scope verify <repo> <id> [--evidence "…"]                       a PERSON measured it against the ask
  cc-scope unverified [<repo>]                                       landed with nothing observed; exit 1 if any
  cc-scope decline <repo> <id> --why "<why it should not be built>"  a decision, kept in the ledger
  cc-scope track|note <repo> <id> "<text>"                           link an ask to a track / add a note
  cc-scope check <repo> [--track T]                                  exit 1 while anything is unsettled
  cc-scope capture <repo> [--src REF] [--who W]                      classify a message on stdin
  cc-scope missed [<repo>] [--since H] [--all] [--full] [--json]     his turns nobody has classified yet
  cc-scope classify [<repo>] [--id T]                                run the classifier over them again
  cc-scope promote <turn> [--track T] [--done-when …] [--check …]    open the ask from the kept text
  cc-scope dismiss <turn> --why "<why it is not an ask>"             it was not an ask, and here is why
  cc-scope digest [<repo>] · count [<repo>] · import <repo> <file> · selfcheck
  cc-scope hook                                                      UserPromptSubmit hook (always 0; speaks only for cc-handoff --relay)

Exit, for the commands a caller acts on: 0 did it · 1 the check ran and FAILED · 2 the row names no check ·
3 the check COULD NOT RUN, so nothing was measured either way.
"""
import sys, os, re, json, time, fcntl, hashlib, calendar, shutil, subprocess

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
SCOPE = os.environ.get("CC_SCOPE_DIR", os.path.join(HOME, ".cc", "scope"))
SEEN = "seen.log"
STATES = ["open", "claimed", "merged", "delivered", "verified", "declined"]
# Off the queue. `merged` is deliberately NOT here — that is the whole fix: code on the default branch is
# still work until something observes it. Declined is settled and STAYS VISIBLE — see the header.
SETTLED = ("delivered", "verified", "declined")
DONE = ("delivered", "verified")     # it actually happened, by a check or by a person's own word
MARK = {"open": "·", "claimed": "▸", "merged": "→", "delivered": "✓", "verified": "✓", "declined": "⊘"}
EXAMPLES = "systemctl --user is-active <unit> · test bin/x -nt <the merge> · a grep of what the tab renders"
CHECK_TIMEOUT = 300  # a verification is meant to be CHEAP: one grep, one systemctl is-active, one curl
MAXTEXT = 800          # a whole essay is not an ask; the source ref is how you read the rest
SEEN_KEEP = 400        # lines of seen.log kept — enough to catch a miss the same day
TURNS = "turns.jsonl"                  # the record: every owner turn in full, written before it is classified
TURNS_ARCHIVE = "turns-archive.jsonl"  # where compaction puts the DISPOSED ones — nothing is ever deleted
TURNS_KEEP = 400                       # records in the live file before disposed ones are archived out of it
TURN_MAX = 100000                      # one turn's text: an ask is not 100 KB, and a paste that size is not
                                       # made more useful by keeping every byte of it forever
# A TOKEN HE PASTED IS NOT KEPT. This record holds what he typed, in full, for as long as nobody disposes of
# it — and a terminal is where people paste credentials. The pattern is cc-slack's own SECRET_RE ("what a
# tool preview relayed to Slack must never carry"), COPIED and not imported: this runs in the
# UserPromptSubmit hook on every prompt, and importing that file to borrow one regex would cost every
# session a parse of it. The selfcheck reads cc-slack's source and holds this copy to it, so it cannot
# drift in silence.
SECRET_RE = re.compile(r"(xox[abpsr]-[A-Za-z0-9-]+|Authorization:\s*\S+(?:\s+\S+)?|Bearer\s+[A-Za-z0-9._~+/=-]{8,}"
                       r"|AKIA[0-9A-Z]{12,}|gh[pousr]_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,})")
# bash's own two codes for "there was nothing to run": 127 not found, 126 found but not executable. A check
# that comes back either of these did not answer the question — see CANNOT RUN in the header.
CANNOT_RUN = (126, 127)
CHECK_TIMED_OUT = 124  # timeout(1)'s code. A check that HUNG did run, and it is a real failure, not silence.
CHECKS = "checks"      # ~/.cc/scope/checks/ — where a check script lives so it outlives the track that wrote it
# A path that dies with the track that made it: a track's own state dir (~/.cc/state/<repo>/<track>/…), a
# worktree, /tmp. Matched only where the command RUNS the thing, so `test ! -d ~/.cc/state/x/y` — a check
# that the janitor cleaned up — is still allowed.
_HOMEISH = r"(?:~|\$HOME|\$\{HOME\}|/home/[^/\s]+|%s)" % re.escape(HOME)
DOOMED = re.compile(r"(?:(?:^|[;&|(])|(?<![\w./-])(?:bash|sh|source|\.)(?=[ \t]))[ \t]*"
                    r"(" + _HOMEISH + r"/\.cc/(?:state/[^/\s]+/[^/\s]+|worktrees)/\S+|/tmp/\S+)")


def now(): return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
def path(repo): return os.path.join(SCOPE, repo + ".json")


class Lock:
    def __init__(self, repo):
        os.makedirs(SCOPE, exist_ok=True)
        self.f = open(os.path.join(SCOPE, repo + ".lock"), "w")
    def __enter__(self): fcntl.flock(self.f, fcntl.LOCK_EX); return self
    def __exit__(self, *a): fcntl.flock(self.f, fcntl.LOCK_UN); self.f.close()


def load(repo):
    try:
        with open(path(repo)) as f: return json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError): return {"repo": repo, "asks": {}}


def save(repo, d):
    os.makedirs(SCOPE, exist_ok=True)
    d["updated"] = now()
    tmp = path(repo) + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f: json.dump(d, f, indent=2)
    os.replace(tmp, path(repo))


def repos():
    try: return sorted(f[:-5] for f in os.listdir(SCOPE) if f.endswith(".json"))
    except OSError: return []


# ---- what counts as an ask -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deterministic, no LLM: the capture path runs on every prompt and must be cheap, silent and predictable.
# It keeps DIRECTIVES — the things that outlive the reply. A plain question is answered in the reply and
# would bury the ledger, so it is logged to seen.log and not opened; `missed` is where you go looking.
_ACKS = (r"thanks?|thx|ty|ok(?:ay)?|k|cool|nice|great|good|sure|yes|yeah|yep|no|nope|perfect|lgtm|"
         r"got ?it|right|agreed|👍|👌|🙏|🎉|❤️")
ACK = re.compile(r"^\W*(?:%s)\b" % _ACKS, re.I)
# AN ACKNOWLEDGEMENT CAN OPEN A REAL INSTRUCTION. A sentence beginning with one used to be dropped whole, so
# "Ok well do whatever you need to speed up the whole landing and review process" (2026-09-08) never
# reached the cue that is plainly in it. The lead is stripped and what follows is judged on its own; what is
# KEPT is still his sentence, ack and all. A bare ack strips to nothing and stays out, and `is_ask` keeps its
# own floor under the short ones ("ok cool, thanks a lot").
ACK_LEAD = re.compile(r"^\W*(?:%s)\b[\s,.:;!—–-]*" % _ACKS, re.I)
CUE_START = re.compile(r"^\W*(add|allow|always|answer|archive|ask|attach|be|break|build|cancel|change|"
                       r"check|clean|close|come|connect|cut|delete|deny|deploy|describe|disable|do|don'?t|"
                       r"drop|enable|ensure|explain|fix|fold|give|go|group|handle|have|hide|hold|install|"
                       r"keep|kill|label|leave|let|link|list|log|look|make|mark|merge|move|name|never|open|"
                       r"ping|post|prefer|print|pull|push|put|react|read|reduce|remind|remove|rename|reply|"
                       r"report|restart|review|rewrite|run|say|send|set|share|show|sort|split|start|stop|"
                       r"switch|take|tell|test|track|try|turn|update|upload|use|verify|wait|write)\b", re.I)
CUE_ANY = re.compile(r"\b(should|shouldn'?t|must|from now on|going forward|i want|i'?d like|i would like|"
                     r"can you|could you|would you|please|needs? to|has to|have to|make sure|instead of|"
                     r"stop \w+ing|no longer|(is|are|was|were|does|do)n'?t work(ing)?|not work(ing)?|i don'?t like|"
                     r"i do not like|not intuitive|rather than)\b", re.I)
SPLIT = re.compile(r"(?<=[.!?;])\s+|\n+")


def without_ack(s, rounds=3):
    """Strip the acknowledgements a sentence leads with: `ok, cool, do X` → `do X`. Bounded, so a sentence
    that is nothing but acks strips down to nothing and carries no cue of its own."""
    for _ in range(rounds):
        t = ACK_LEAD.sub("", s, count=1).strip()
        if t == s: break
        s = t
    return s


def parts_of(text):
    """The directive sentences inside a message. A compound ask is where scope goes missing, so they are
    kept separately and shown by `show` — the row is the message, the parts are what it actually asked."""
    out = []
    for s in SPLIT.split(text):
        s = s.strip(" -*·\t")
        if len(s.split()) < 2: continue
        core = without_ack(s)
        # CUE_START on what is left once the ack is off; CUE_ANY on the WHOLE sentence, because a cue can
        # begin with an ack word ("no longer …") and stripping it would lose one.
        if core and (CUE_START.match(core) or CUE_ANY.search(s)): out.append(s[:200])
    return out


def is_ask(text):
    t = text.strip()
    if not t or t.startswith(("!", "/")): return False        # a free ! command / slash command is not an ask
    if len(t.split()) < 3 and ACK.match(t): return False
    if ACK.match(t) and len(t.split()) < 6: return False       # "thanks, that looks great"
    return bool(parts_of(t))


def norm(text):
    return re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9 ]", "", re.sub(r"\s+", " ", text.lower())).strip()


def fingerprint(text): return hashlib.sha1(norm(text).encode()).hexdigest()[:12]


# ---- the ledger ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
def add(repo, text, who="owner", src="", track="", done_when="", check="", at="", replay=False):
    """Returns (id, repeated). The same ask made twice does not open a second row — it bumps `repeats`,
    which is the signal that matters: 'asked twice, no answer yet' is a line from a real handoff. Asked
    again after it was DECLINED, it goes back on the queue (the reason stays); asked again after it was
    VERIFIED it is a new row, because that is a regression, not a repeat. `replay` (import) matches every
    status and changes nothing — re-running a seed file must be a no-op, not a second ledger."""
    text = " ".join(text.split())[:MAXTEXT]
    if not text: return None, False
    fp = fingerprint(text)
    with Lock(repo):
        d = load(repo)
        for a in d["asks"].values():
            if a["fp"] != fp: continue
            if replay: return a["id"], True
            if a["status"] in DONE and settled(a): continue
            a["repeats"] = a.get("repeats", 1) + 1
            a.setdefault("seen", []).append({"ts": at or now(), "src": src})
            if a["status"] == "declined":
                a["status"] = "open"; a["closed"] = ""
                a["notes"].append({"ts": now(), "msg": "asked again after it was declined — back on the "
                                                       "queue, with the reason it was declined kept above"})
            save(repo, d)
            return a["id"], True
        n = 1
        while "a%d" % n in d["asks"]: n += 1
        aid = "a%d" % n
        d["asks"][aid] = {"id": aid, "text": text, "parts": parts_of(text), "fp": fp, "who": who,
                          "src": src, "track": track, "done_when": done_when, "status": "open",
                          "asked": at or now(), "repeats": 1, "evidence": "", "reason": "", "closed": "",
                          "check": check.strip(), "checked": {}, "notes": []}
        save(repo, d)
        return aid, False


# ---- the verification: a command, not a sentence ---------------------------------------------------
def repo_dir(repo):
    """Where a row's check runs. The repo's own checkout if there is one, so a check can say `test -x bin/x`;
    otherwise the home directory, which is where a deployed thing actually lives."""
    p = os.path.join(HOME, "dev", repo)
    return p if os.path.isdir(p) else HOME


def has_check(a): return bool((a.get("check") or "").strip())


def passed(a): return (a.get("checked") or {}).get("rc") == 0


def cannot_run(a):
    """The check did not answer. Everything above this line saw one rc≠0 and called it "the check has not
    passed", which is how a69 read as a red boundary check for five days after its script went with its
    track's state dir. No answer is not the same as the answer no — but "no answer" has more than one cause
    (see shell_could_not_exec below), and this only tells CANNOT_RUN apart from a real failure, not one
    CANNOT_RUN cause from another."""
    return (a.get("checked") or {}).get("rc") in CANNOT_RUN


# bash's OWN diagnostic when it never found anything to run: always starts the line "bash: " and ends in one
# of its three fixed reasons. A check can return the SAME rc on purpose, though — a69's rebuilt boundary check
# exits 126 itself to say some of what it would measure never started (2026-09-07, no member/credential words
# here on purpose: this file must not learn what any one check means). The interpreter ran that check fine, so
# this text never appears in its output, and telling the reader to "give it a command that is there" would be
# advice for a problem it does not have. Generic and mechanical: it reads bash's own wording, not the check's.
_SHELL_EXEC_FAILURE = re.compile(r"^bash: .*(?:No such file or directory|Permission denied|command not found)",
                                 re.M)


def shell_could_not_exec(output):
    return bool(_SHELL_EXEC_FAILURE.search(output or ""))


def check_dir():
    d = os.path.join(SCOPE, CHECKS)
    os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
    return d


def refuse_doomed(repo, aid, c):
    """A check is read months after it is written, long after the track that wrote it was cleaned up. This
    refusal used to live inside the check-cmd branch alone, which left `add --check` as a documented door to
    exactly the row it exists to prevent (review of #297)."""
    m = DOOMED.search(c or "")
    if m:
        sys.exit("cc-scope: that check RUNS a script out of %s, which does not outlive the track that "
                 "made it.\n  A track's state dir, its worktree and /tmp are all temporary by design, "
                 "and when one goes\n  the row reads as a failing check when in fact nothing has been "
                 "measured (a69, 2026-09-02 to 09-07).\n  Keep the script beside the ledger instead:\n"
                 "    cc-scope check-cmd %s %s --script %s" % (m.group(1), repo, aid, m.group(1)))


def install_script(repo, aid, src):
    """Copy a check script in beside the LEDGER. A track's state dir and its worktree are both temporary by
    design — the janitor takes them when the track is done — so a check that lives in one is a check with an
    expiry date on it. This is the durable place: the ledger's own dir, keyed by repo and ask."""
    src = os.path.expanduser(src)
    if not os.path.isfile(src):
        sys.exit("cc-scope: --script %s is not a file. Point it at the script as it is now: check-cmd copies "
                 "it in, so the original may be in a track dir that later goes away." % src)
    dst = os.path.join(check_dir(), "%s.%s.sh" % (repo, aid))
    shutil.copyfile(src, dst)
    os.chmod(dst, 0o755)
    return dst


def settled(a):
    """Off the queue. `delivered` counts only if a check ACTUALLY PASSED. Every row this code delivers has
    one by construction — but rows written before it exists sit in `delivered` on sentences like "merged as
    PR #64 on main; deployed", which is the precise thing that is no longer evidence. Promoting those to
    settled because the word matches would close five real asks nobody ever measured, which is the failure
    this whole file is here to stop, committed by the fix for it. They go back on the queue instead."""
    if a.get("status") == "delivered":
        return passed(a)
    return a.get("status") in SETTLED


def record_undelivered(repo, a, rc, out):
    """A MERGED FIX THAT STILL FAILS ITS CHECK IS A FAILURE, and the check runner that saw it writes it to the failures
    ledger (docs/design-failure-noticing-2026-09-11.md, phase 2): kind delivery/merged-not-delivered, through
    `cc-green red`, ONCE per ask and merge — the key carries the merge's evidence, so the next tick on the same
    still-red ask writes nothing (`already`) and a new merge that still fails is a new record. Seven such rows were
    live on 2026-09-11 and nothing counted them. Never a verdict: the row's state and this command's exit code were
    decided before this ran, and a ledger that cannot be written is one line on stderr, not an error."""
    try:
        import tempfile
        key = "scope-deliver:%s:%s:%s" % (repo, a["id"], hashlib.sha1((a.get("evidence") or "").encode()).hexdigest()[:8])
        with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile("w", prefix="cc-scope-check.", suffix=".out", delete=False) as f:
            f.write(out or ""); log = f.name
        try:
            r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-green"), "red", repo,
                                "delivery/merged-not-delivered", key,
                                "--observed", ("ask %s of %s is merged (%s) and its check still fails (rc %d): %s \u2014 %s"
                                               % (a["id"], repo, a.get("evidence") or "no evidence", rc, a["check"],
                                                  " ".join((out or "").split())[-200:] or "(no output)"))[:600],
                                "--expected", "a merged fix passes the check its ask names", "--log", log,
                                "--writer", "cc-scope", "--link", "ask=%s" % a["id"], "--link", "check=%s" % a["check"][:200]]
                               + (["--link", "track=%s" % a["track"]] if a.get("track") else []),
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
            word = ((r.stdout.strip() or r.stderr.strip()).splitlines() or ["not recorded: cc-green red said nothing"])[-1]
        finally:
            try: os.unlink(log)
            except OSError: pass
    except Exception as e:   # the ledger never decides a delivery
        word = "not recorded: %s: %s" % (e.__class__.__name__, e)
    print("cc-scope: failures ledger (%s): %s" % (repo, word), file=sys.stderr)


def run_check(repo, a):
    """(rc, output). The EXIT CODE is the whole answer — a check prints for the human reading the failure,
    and decides nothing by what it prints. Anything that goes wrong running it is a failure, not a pass:
    the one direction this must never err in is towards 'delivered'. Failure is not one thing, though:
    126/127 mean the command was never there to answer (CANNOT_RUN), and a hang means it ran and would not
    finish, so a timeout comes back as its own code rather than borrowing the one for 'not found'."""
    env = dict(os.environ)
    # A check names the box's own tools the way a person would at a prompt (`cc-limit selfcheck`), so they have to
    # BE on PATH when it runs. The landing queue's worker has a PATH without the user's bin dir, and three checks
    # in a row died there with rc=127 while the same command passed by hand (2026-09-02) — a tooling failure read
    # as "not delivered". The dir this file lives in is that bin dir, so prepend it and let a check stay readable.
    # os.defpath is the floor: passing env= makes subprocess resolve the INTERPRETER through this PATH too, so an
    # inherited PATH that is empty or broken would lose `bash` itself and every check would read as rc=127.
    env["PATH"] = os.pathsep.join([os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), env.get("PATH", "") or os.defpath])
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["bash", "-c", a["check"]], cwd=repo_dir(repo), capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=CHECK_TIMEOUT, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, env=env)
        return r.returncode, r.stdout + r.stderr
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return CHECK_TIMED_OUT, "the check did not finish inside %ds" % CHECK_TIMEOUT
    except Exception as e:
        return 127, "%s: %s" % (e.__class__.__name__, e)


def get(repo, aid):
    a = load(repo)["asks"].get(aid)
    if not a: sys.exit("cc-scope: %s has no ask '%s'" % (repo, aid))
    return a


def edit(repo, aid, fn):
    with Lock(repo):
        d = load(repo)
        if aid not in d["asks"]: sys.exit("cc-scope: %s has no ask '%s'" % (repo, aid))
        fn(d["asks"][aid]); save(repo, d)


def select(repo, status=None, track=None, everything=False):
    """No status named = the unsettled ones: what is still owed. A declined ask is settled — it is a decision
    already taken, so it stops being work — but `--all` and `--status declined` still show it, with its reason."""
    asks = sorted(load(repo)["asks"].values(), key=lambda a: a["asked"])
    if not everything:
        asks = [a for a in asks if (a["status"] == status if status else not settled(a))]
    if track: asks = [a for a in asks if a.get("track") == track]
    return asks


def line(repo, a, many=False):
    flag = " ⚠ asked %d×" % a["repeats"] if a.get("repeats", 1) > 1 else ""
    tail = " [%s]" % a["track"] if a.get("track") else ""
    head = "%s/" % repo if many else ""
    why = "\n       ⊘ %s" % a["reason"] if a["status"] == "declined" and a.get("reason") else ""
    # A merged row is the one the ledger used to call done. It says so on the line you actually read, because
    # "has home been updated yet?" is the question this is here to answer without anyone having to go and look.
    if a["status"] == "merged":
        why = "\n       ⚠ merged, NOT delivered — %s" % (
            "its check CANNOT RUN, so nothing has been measured either way — not a red check: %s" % a["check"]
            if cannot_run(a) else "its check has not passed: %s" % a["check"] if has_check(a)
            else "it names no check, so nothing has observed it")
    elif a["status"] == "delivered" and not passed(a):
        why = "\n       ⚠ NOT delivered — closed on a sentence before a check was required, so it is back on the queue"
    return "%s %s%-4s %s%s%s%s" % (MARK.get(a["status"], "?"), head, a["id"], a["text"][:96], tail, flag, why)


# ---- capture ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TAG = re.compile(r"<channel\s([^>]*)>", re.S)
ATTR = re.compile(r'(\w+)="([^"]*)"')


def strip_tag(text):
    """Slack arrives wrapped in a <channel …> tag. Returns (body, attrs) — the tag carries who is speaking
    (role=owner|member) and where, both of which decide whether this is an owner ask and how to cite it."""
    m = TAG.search(text)
    if not m: return text.strip(), {}
    attrs = dict(ATTR.findall(m.group(1)))
    body = (text[:m.start()] + text[m.end():])
    body = re.sub(r"</channel>", "", body).strip()
    return body, attrs


def log_seen(repo, decision, text, src):
    try:
        os.makedirs(SCOPE, exist_ok=True)
        p = os.path.join(SCOPE, SEEN)
        with open(p, "a") as f:
            f.write("%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n" % (now(), repo, decision, src or "-",
                                              " ".join(text.split())[:160]))
        with open(p) as f: lines = f.readlines()
        if len(lines) > SEEN_KEEP * 2:                      # trim in batches, not on every write
            with open(p + ".tmp", "w") as f: f.writelines(lines[-SEEN_KEEP:])
            os.replace(p + ".tmp", p)
    except OSError: pass


# ---- the record: kept before it is classified ------------------------------------------------------
# seen.log is a trail for a person to read: one line per turn, clipped at 160 characters and trimmed to the
# last SEEN_KEEP lines — which is how the only copy of a real ask went missing. This is the record itself:
# full text, who, where, and what was eventually decided about it. Nothing leaves it for being old.

def redact(text):
    """Tokens out, before anything writes the turn down. Idempotent — running it twice changes nothing."""
    return SECRET_RE.sub("[redacted]", text or "")


def turns_path(name=TURNS): return os.path.join(SCOPE, name)


def read_turns(name=TURNS):
    out = []
    try:
        with open(turns_path(name)) as f:
            for ln in f:
                ln = ln.strip()
                if not ln: continue
                try: out.append(json.loads(ln))
                except ValueError: continue        # one half-written line is not a reason to lose the rest
    except OSError: pass
    return out


def write_turns(recs):
    tmp = turns_path() + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        for r in recs: f.write(json.dumps(r) + "\n")
    os.replace(tmp, turns_path())


def compact_turns():
    """Move the DISPOSED records to the archive and keep every pending one. The caller holds the lock.
    A file that is all pending simply grows: turns nobody has classified are a backlog, and trimming them
    by age is the bug this record exists to end."""
    recs = read_turns()
    if len(recs) <= TURNS_KEEP: return 0
    gone = [r for r in recs if r.get("disposed")]
    if not gone: return 0
    with open(turns_path(TURNS_ARCHIVE), "a") as f:
        for r in gone: f.write(json.dumps(r) + "\n")
    write_turns([r for r in recs if not r.get("disposed")])
    return len(gone)


def keep_turn(repo, text, who="owner", src=""):
    """Write one owner turn down, in full, before anything judges it. Returns its id, or "" if it could not
    be written — the hook must never break a session, so this raises nothing."""
    try:
        os.makedirs(SCOPE, exist_ok=True)
        text = redact(text)                             # every caller's text, not just the capture path's
        if len(text) > TURN_MAX:
            text = text[:TURN_MAX] + " … [%d characters not kept: one turn over %d]" % (
                len(text) - TURN_MAX, TURN_MAX)
        ts = now()
        with Lock("turns"):
            p = turns_path()
            seq = os.path.getsize(p) if os.path.exists(p) else 0
            tid = "t" + hashlib.sha1(("%s|%s|%d" % (ts, text, seq)).encode()).hexdigest()[:8]
            with open(p, "a") as f:
                f.write(json.dumps({"id": tid, "ts": ts, "repo": repo, "who": who, "src": src,
                                    "text": text, "disposed": "", "why": "", "ask": ""}) + "\n")
            compact_turns()
        return tid
    except OSError: return ""


def dispose_turn(tid, how, why="", ask=""):
    """What the turn turned out to be: classified, promoted or dismissed. A record already disposed of keeps
    the first answer given — this is a log of what was decided, not a field to overwrite."""
    try:
        with Lock("turns"):
            recs = read_turns()
            for r in recs:
                if r.get("id") == tid and not r.get("disposed"):
                    r.update(disposed=how, why=why, ask=ask, closed=now())
                    write_turns(recs)
                    return True
    except OSError: pass
    return False


def get_turn(tid):
    for name in (TURNS, TURNS_ARCHIVE):
        for r in read_turns(name):
            if r.get("id") == tid: return r
    sys.exit("cc-scope: no kept turn %s — `cc-scope missed --all` lists the ones still waiting" % tid)


def turns_size():
    """(pending, rows, bytes) of the live record. Nothing ages out of that file, so the one place it is read
    is the place that has to say how big it has become — before it is somebody's surprise."""
    try: b = os.path.getsize(turns_path())
    except OSError: b = 0
    recs = read_turns()
    return sum(1 for r in recs if not r.get("disposed")), len(recs), b


def human(b):
    for u in ("B", "KB", "MB", "GB"):
        if b < 1024 or u == "GB": return "%d %s" % (b, u) if u == "B" else "%.1f %s" % (b, u)
        b /= 1024.0


def pending_turns(repo=None, since=0.0):
    """The turns nobody has answered for yet, oldest first."""
    out = []
    for r in read_turns():
        if r.get("disposed"): continue
        if repo and r.get("repo") != repo: continue
        if since:
            try:
                if calendar.timegm(time.strptime(r.get("ts", ""), "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")) < since: continue
            except ValueError: continue
        out.append(r)
    return out


# The Slack path: a channel message reaches the session as a user turn, so the UserPromptSubmit hook sees it
# with its <channel …> tag intact and `capture` takes the rest from there — no hook inside cc-slack. If a
# delivery ever bypasses that turn, one line in cc-slack's deliver() closes the gap and nothing else changes:
#   subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-scope", "capture", repo, "--src", f"slack {chan} {thread_ts}"], input=text, text=True)
# A REACTION CARRIES NO WORDS OF HIS. cc-slack hands a session a 👍 on one of ITS OWN messages as a message
# of its own — `👍 on your 13:26 reply: "…"`, tagged kind="reaction" — and everything after the colon is our
# reply quoted back, up to sixty characters of it. The classifier read those sixty characters as the owner's
# and opened a row: one on our own "Fix on its way", two more on our own "rather than". All three were
# read and declined by hand, and while they sat there they counted toward the open-ask number the pulse
# reports. Most 👍 miss the cues and are skipped as no-directive, which is why this looked intermittent —
# it was never the reaction, it was whichever of our sentences the excerpt happened to cut. So it is decided
# on the tag, which is the fact, and never on the shape of the quote: approving what was already said is the
# whole of a reaction, and a 👎 or a removal says just as little that belongs in a ledger of requests. It is
# still logged, because it IS the owner speaking and `missed` is where a wrong call gets found.
REACTION = "reaction"


def capture(repo, text, who="owner", src=""):
    """PROVENANCE, THEN KEEP, THEN CLASSIFY — in that order, because the classifier is the part that has
    been wrong. Everything the provenance rules accept as his is on disk in full before `is_ask` is asked
    anything, and a turn it skips stays pending in that record instead of surviving only as a clipped log
    line. What provenance excludes — a member, a machine, a reaction, which carries no words of his — is not
    kept at all: this is a record of what the OWNER said, and padding it makes it useless."""
    body, attrs = strip_tag(text)
    body = redact(body)                                 # before the record, the ledger and seen.log alike
    if attrs:
        if attrs.get("role") and attrs["role"] != "owner":
            log_seen(repo, "skip:not-owner", body, src); return None, False   # the ledger is the OWNER's asks
        who = attrs.get("user") or who
        if not src and attrs.get("channel"):
            src = "slack %s %s" % (attrs["channel"], attrs.get("thread_ts") or attrs.get("ts") or "")
        if attrs.get("kind") == REACTION:
            log_seen(repo, "skip:reaction", body, src); return None, False    # our own reply, quoted back
    if not_a_person(text, attrs): return None, False    # the hook's own gate, and the same one for `capture`
    tid = keep_turn(repo, body, who, src.strip())
    if not is_ask(body):
        log_seen(repo, "skip:no-directive", body, src)
        return None, False                              # the record stays pending until somebody disposes of it
    aid, repeated = add(repo, body, who=who, src=src.strip())
    dispose_turn(tid, "classified", ask="%s %s" % (repo, aid))
    log_seen(repo, "kept:%s%s" % (aid, "+" if repeated else ""), body, src)
    return aid, repeated


def repo_of(cwd):
    """A session's repo from its cwd: ~/dev/<repo> or ~/.cc/worktrees/<repo>/<track>."""
    cwd = os.path.realpath(cwd or "")
    for base, depth in ((os.path.join(HOME, "dev"), 1), (os.path.join(HOME, ".cc", "worktrees"), 1)):
        base = os.path.realpath(base)
        if cwd == base or cwd.startswith(base + os.sep):
            rest = cwd[len(base):].strip(os.sep).split(os.sep)
            if rest and rest[0]: return rest[0]
    return ""


def worker_cwd(cwd):
    """A track worktree (marker .cc/track) is a WORKER: its prompts are the task it was dispatched with,
    not asks the owner made. Same marker cc-guard walks, for the same reason — the marker is the truth."""
    d = os.path.realpath(cwd or "")
    while d and d != "/":
        if os.path.exists(os.path.join(d, ".cc", "track")): return True
        d = os.path.dirname(d)
    return False


# ---- what is not a person asking ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below reached UserPromptSubmit as a user turn and opened a row somebody then had to decline by
# hand (a28, a29, a54, a60, a61, a63 on 2026-08-31). The ledger is the authority on what the OWNER asked
# for, so a wrong row costs more than a missed one. Matched on the start of the prompt — every one of these
# wrappers leads — and NOT written to seen.log: that file is 400 lines of owner messages the classifier may
# have got wrong, and one night of task-notifications is enough to flush a real miss out of it.
NOT_A_PERSON = (
    "<task-notification",           # the harness reporting a background task or subagent finished
    "<cross-session-message",       # a peer session writing into this one over its socket
    "[SYSTEM NOTIFICATION",         # the harness in its own voice: limits, restarts, context
    "[Cross-session idle notice]",  # the idle sweep saying another session went quiet
    "<command-name",                # a slash command the session ran — the invocation, not an ask
    "<local-command-stdout",        # ...and its output, handed back as the next turn
    "[cc-handoff]",                 # a prompt cc-handoff typed into a session: it tags every one (its MACHINE)
)

# The harness also speaks in plain sentences, with no wrapper to lead with, and those turns carry real
# imperatives: "Your claude.ai usage limit has reset. Continue the task you were working on…" opened a row
# in FOUR repos' ledgers within a minute of the same reset on 2026-09-04, one of them splitting out a
# sub-directive, and every one was declined by hand. Only the wording gives these away, so they are matched at
# the start of the prompt and loosely — the harness rewords them between versions — and never mid-sentence,
# which is what keeps an owner ask ABOUT the limit ("the usage limit keeps eating the queue, fix it") a row.
HARNESS_NOTICE = re.compile(r"""(?ix) ^\s* (
      (your\ \S+\ )? usage\ limit\ has\ reset                # the reset that wakes a stopped session back up
    | (claude\ )? usage\ limit\ reached                      # the limit itself, and its auto-continue banner
    | [\w.\ ]{0,20} limit\ reached [^\n]{0,40}? now\ using  # a reroute: "Opus limit reached · now using Sonnet"
    | this\ session\ is\ being\ continued                    # the banner a resumed session opens with
    | continue\ from\ where\ you\ left\ off                  # ...and the bare nudge that resumes one
)""")


def not_a_person(text, attrs):
    """True when this prompt was written by a machine. `attrs` is the <channel …> tag if there was one:
    a Slack message is an owner ask only when a real owner really typed it in a real channel. `inject` is a
    local, unauthenticated door any process on the box may use and it stamps role="owner" chat_id="local",
    which is how the board watchdog's own line became an owner ask (a54)."""
    if text.lstrip().startswith(NOT_A_PERSON): return True
    if HARNESS_NOTICE.match(text): return True
    if attrs:
        if attrs.get("role") != "owner": return True                        # member, bot, anything but him
        if attrs.get("chat_id") == "local" or attrs.get("channel") == "local": return True   # injected here
        if attrs.get("source", "cc-slack") != "cc-slack": return True       # absent = the ordinary Slack path
    return False


def relayed(payload):
    """`cc-handoff --relay`, with the hook's own payload: it answers with a block decision (JSON on stdout) when
    this session is the RETIRING side of a handoff and the prompt went to its successor, and with nothing
    otherwise. Its stdout is handed back as ours; any failure is no decision — the hook must never break a session."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-handoff"), "--relay"],
                           input=json.dumps(payload), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
        if r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout.strip():
            sys.stdout.write(r.stdout); sys.stdout.flush()
            return True
    except Exception: pass
    return False


def hook():
    """UserPromptSubmit. Prints NOTHING of its own (stdout would land in the session's context) and always exits
    0: a ledger that can break a session is worse than no ledger. The one thing it passes through is cc-handoff's
    --relay decision — a prompt typed into a retiring session goes to its successor and is blocked here."""
    try:
        payload = json.load(sys.stdin)
        text = payload.get("prompt") or ""
        cwd = payload.get("cwd") or os.getcwd()
        if not text.strip(): return
        if relayed(payload): return                          # the successor's ask now, not this session's
        _, attrs = strip_tag(text)
        if not_a_person(text, attrs): return                 # machine chatter, not an ask — see NOT_A_PERSON
        if not attrs:                                        # no channel tag: a prompt typed into the session
            # "steward" too: cc-steward's one-shot prompt is a machine's event, not a person's ask, and it arrives
            # with no channel tag — captured, it would file the event as an ask against repo "box".
            if os.environ.get("CC_ROLE") in ("worker", "member", "steward") or worker_cwd(cwd): return
        repo = repo_of(cwd) or os.environ.get("CC_REPO") or "box"
        src = "slack" if attrs else "terminal %s" % (payload.get("session_id") or "")[:8]
        capture(repo, text, src="" if attrs else src)
    except Exception: pass


# ---- commands --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
def opt(args, name, default=""):
    if name in args:
        i = args.index(name)
        v = args[i + 1] if len(args) > i + 1 else ""
        del args[i:i + 2]
        return v
    return default


def flag(args, name):
    if name in args: args.remove(name); return True
    return False


def main(argv):
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help"): sys.exit(__doc__)
    cmd, args = argv[0], list(argv[1:])
    js = flag(args, "--json")

    if cmd == "add":
        repo, text = args[0], args[1]
        # ONE read of --check, reused: opt() DELETES the option and its value out of args, so asking twice
        # hands the second caller "" and the row silently stores no check at all (review of #297).
        chk = opt(args, "--check")
        refuse_doomed(repo, "<new>", chk)   # the same door as check-cmd, and it was open here
        aid, rep = add(repo, text, who=opt(args, "--who", "owner"), src=opt(args, "--src"),
                       track=opt(args, "--track"), done_when=opt(args, "--done-when"),
                       check=chk, at=opt(args, "--at"))
        print("%s %s%s" % (repo, aid, " (asked again)" if rep else ""))

    elif cmd == "capture":
        repo = args[0]
        aid, rep = capture(repo, sys.stdin.read(), who=opt(args, "--who", "owner"), src=opt(args, "--src"))
        print("%s %s" % (repo, aid + (" (asked again)" if rep else "")) if aid else "not an ask (logged)")

    elif cmd == "list":
        status, track, everything = opt(args, "--status") or None, opt(args, "--track") or None, flag(args, "--all")
        rs = [args[0]] if args else repos()
        rows = [(r, a) for r in rs for a in select(r, status, track, everything)]
        if js: print(json.dumps([a for _, a in rows], indent=2)); return
        if not rows: print("no asks" if everything else "nothing %s" % (status or "unsettled")); return
        for r, a in rows: print(line(r, a, many=len(rs) > 1))

    elif cmd == "show":
        a = get(args[0], args[1])
        print("%s %s [%s]%s" % (args[0], a["id"], a["status"],
                                "  ⚠ asked %d×" % a["repeats"] if a.get("repeats", 1) > 1 else ""))
        print("  asked   %s by %s%s" % (a["asked"], a["who"], "  (%s)" % a["src"] if a["src"] else ""))
        if a.get("track"):     print("  track   %s" % a["track"])
        if a.get("done_when"): print("  done when %s" % a["done_when"])
        if a.get("reason"):    print("  declined because %s" % a["reason"])
        if has_check(a):
            # The check and what it last DID, on the same two lines: the notes below carry every run, and
            # "has this been measured at all?" should not need reading them in order.
            c = a.get("checked") or {}
            print("  check   %s" % a["check"])
            print("          %s" % ("never run" if not c else
                                    "passed %s" % c["ts"] if c.get("rc") == 0 else
                                    "COULD NOT RUN %s (rc %d) — nothing measured" % (c["ts"], c["rc"])
                                    if c.get("rc") in CANNOT_RUN else
                                    "ran and FAILED %s (rc %d)" % (c["ts"], c["rc"])))
        print("  %s" % a["text"])
        for p in a.get("parts", []): print("    – %s" % p)
        for s in a.get("seen", []): print("  again   %s %s" % (s["ts"], s.get("src", "") or "-"))
        if a["evidence"]: print("  evidence %s%s" % (a["evidence"], "  (%s)" % a["closed"] if a["closed"] else ""))
        for n in a["notes"]: print("  note    %s %s" % (n["ts"], n["msg"]))

    elif cmd == "check-cmd":
        script = opt(args, "--script")
        repo, aid = args[0], args[1]
        get(repo, aid)     # before anything is copied anywhere
        if script:
            c = "bash %s" % install_script(repo, aid, script)
        else:
            c = (args[2] if len(args) > 2 else "").strip()
            if not c: sys.exit("cc-scope: check-cmd needs a command — one that exits 0 only when the ask is TRUE")
            refuse_doomed(repo, aid, c)
        edit(repo, aid, lambda a: a.update(check=c, checked={}))
        print("%s %s check: %s" % (repo, aid, c))

    elif cmd == "deliver":
        # THE HOLE THIS CLOSES: this used to take `--evidence "merged as PR #N"` and believe it. It now runs
        # the row's own check and believes only the exit code. There is no argument that can talk it round.
        repo, aid = args[0], args[1]
        track, ev = opt(args, "--track"), opt(args, "--evidence")
        a = get(repo, aid)
        if track: edit(repo, aid, lambda x: x.update(track=track))
        if not has_check(a):
            print("cc-scope: %s %s names no check — UNVERIFIED. It stays '%s' and it stays on the queue: a merge\n"
                  "  is not delivery, and nothing here has observed this reach anyone. Give it one —\n"
                  "    cc-scope check-cmd %s %s '<command that exits 0 only when the ask is true>'\n"
                  "  (%s)\n"
                  "  or, if no mechanical check can exist, put your own name on it and say what you looked at:\n"
                  "    cc-scope verify %s %s --evidence \"...\"" % (repo, aid, a["status"], repo, aid, EXAMPLES,
                                                                   repo, aid),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        rc, o = run_check(repo, a)
        tail = " ".join((o or "").split())[-300:]
        silent = rc in CANNOT_RUN     # no answer, which is not the answer no
        def upd(x):
            rec = {"ts": now(), "cmd": x["check"], "rc": rc, "out": tail}
            if rc == 0:
                x["checked"] = rec
                x["status"], x["closed"] = "delivered", now()
                x["evidence"] = ((ev + " \u00b7 ") if ev else "") + "check passed: " + x["check"]
            elif silent:
                x["notes"].append({"ts": now(), "msg": "check COULD NOT RUN (rc %d) %s \u2014 %s"
                                                      % (rc, x["check"], tail[:160])})
                # A check that was never asked must not erase the answer of one that was: overwriting a green
                # record with rc=127 would drag a delivered row back onto the queue because a FILE went away.
                if not passed(x): x["checked"] = rec
            else:
                x["checked"] = rec
                x["notes"].append({"ts": now(), "msg": "check FAILED (rc %d) %s \u2014 %s" % (rc, x["check"], tail[:160])})
        edit(repo, aid, upd)
        if rc and not silent and a.get("status") == "merged":
            record_undelivered(repo, a, rc, o)   # "a merged fix still fails its check": the ledger's line, once per merge
        if silent:
            if shell_could_not_exec(o):
                # bash itself never found anything to run — the vanished-script cause #297 was written for.
                fix = ("  Give it a command that is there, or keep the script beside the ledger where it "
                       "outlives\n  the track that wrote it:  cc-scope check-cmd %s %s --script <file>"
                       % (repo, aid))
            else:
                # The interpreter ran the check fine; the check chose this rc itself to say it could not
                # measure something. That is not a missing command, so this must not send the reader to fix
                # a path that was never broken — the reason is whatever it printed above, on the check.
                fix = ("  That is not a missing command \u2014 read what it printed above for the reason, and "
                       "rerun\n  once it changes:  cc-scope deliver %s %s" % (repo, aid))
            print("cc-scope: %s %s \u2014 its check COULD NOT RUN (rc %d). This is NOT a failed check: nothing was\n"
                  "  measured, so the ask has not been answered either way%s.\n  %s\n  %s\n%s"
                  % (repo, aid, rc, " and the row stays '%s' on the run that did pass" % a["status"]
                     if passed(a) else "", a["check"], tail[:200] or "(no output)", fix),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        if rc:
            print("cc-scope: %s %s is NOT delivered — its check failed (rc %d), so it stays '%s' and stays open.\n"
                  "  %s\n  %s" % (repo, aid, rc, a["status"], a["check"], tail[:200] or "(no output)"),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        print("%s %s delivered — its check passed: %s" % (repo, aid, a["check"]))

    elif cmd in ("claim", "merged", "verify", "declined", "decline", "done", "drop"):
        state = {"claim": "claimed", "merged": "merged", "verify": "verified", "done": "verified",
                 "decline": "declined", "declined": "declined", "drop": "declined"}[cmd]
        repo, aid = args[0], args[1]
        track, ev, why = opt(args, "--track"), opt(args, "--evidence"), opt(args, "--why")
        a = load(repo)["asks"].get(aid, {})
        if state == "merged":
            if not ev:
                sys.exit("cc-scope: merged needs --evidence — the PR or commit that landed")
            if a.get("status") in DONE:   # a re-run must never drag a delivered row backwards
                print("%s %s is already %s — a re-merge does not undo it" % (repo, aid, a["status"])); return 0
        if state == "verified":
            # A person may close what no machine can measure. They may NOT overrule a check they themselves wrote:
            # a red check with a confident sentence beside it is the pair that closed two rows the owner then reopened.
            if has_check(a) and not passed(a):
                sys.exit("cc-scope: %s names a check and it has not passed — the ledger runs the check, it does "
                         "not take a sentence for it.\n  %s\nRun it (`cc-scope deliver %s %s`), or replace it "
                         "with one that can pass (`cc-scope check-cmd %s %s ...`)."
                         % (aid, a["check"], repo, aid, repo, aid))
            if not (ev or a.get("evidence")):
                sys.exit("cc-scope: verify needs --evidence — name what YOU looked at, in your own words. This is "
                         "the by-hand path for an ask no command can measure, so a PR number is not an answer.")
        if state == "declined" and not why:   # the reason IS the decision: without one this is a drop, and a drop
            sys.exit("cc-scope: decline needs --why — a decline without a reason is the drop this ledger exists "
                     "to catch. Say, in your own words, why it should not be built.")
        def upd(a):
            a["status"] = state
            if track: a["track"] = track
            if ev: a["evidence"] = ev
            if why: a["reason"] = why
            a["closed"] = now() if state in SETTLED else ""
        edit(repo, aid, upd)
        print("%s %s %s%s" % (repo, aid, state,
              " — NOT delivered: nothing has observed this yet" if state == "merged" else ""))

    elif cmd == "unverified":
        # The honest column. Every row the box has said landed, where no check has ever gone green: the merged
        # ones still owed, and the ones a person closed on their own word. Both are real; neither was measured.
        rs = [args[0]] if args else repos()
        rows = [(r, a) for r in rs for a in select(r, None, opt(args, "--track") or None, everything=True)
                if a["status"] in ("merged", "delivered", "verified") and not passed(a)]
        if js: print(json.dumps([a for _, a in rows], indent=2)); return 1 if rows else 0
        if not rows:
            print("cc-scope: nothing unverified — everything the box says landed was checked, and it passed")
            return 0
        print("cc-scope: %d ask(s) the box says landed with nothing observed at the other end:" % len(rows))
        for r, a in rows:
            if a["status"] == "merged":
                why = ("STILL OPEN, names no check — cc-scope check-cmd %s %s '<cmd>'" % (r, a["id"])
                       if not has_check(a) else
                       "STILL OPEN and NEVER MEASURED — its check CANNOT RUN, which is not a red check: %s"
                       % a["check"] if cannot_run(a) else
                       "STILL OPEN, its check has not passed: %s" % a["check"])
            elif a["status"] == "delivered":   # written before a check was required — a sentence, not a measurement
                why = "STILL OPEN, closed on a sentence: %s" % (a.get("evidence") or "no evidence")
            else:   # verified: a person's own word, which is allowed and is not the same as measured
                why = "closed by hand, never measured: %s" % (a.get("evidence") or "no evidence")
            print("  %-4s %s%s" % (a["id"], "%s/" % r if len(rs) > 1 else "", a["text"][:88]))
            print("       %s" % why)
        return 1

    elif cmd == "track":
        edit(args[0], args[1], lambda a: a.update(track=args[2])); print("%s %s → %s" % tuple(args[:3]))

    elif cmd == "note":
        edit(args[0], args[1], lambda a: a["notes"].append({"ts": now(), "msg": args[2]}))
        print("%s %s noted" % (args[0], args[1]))

    elif cmd == "check":          # 'done' measured against the asks: exit 1 while anything is still open
        repo = args[0]; track = opt(args, "--track") or None
        open_ = select(repo, None, track)
        where = " for %s" % track if track else ""
        if not open_: print("cc-scope: nothing unsettled%s" % where); return 0
        m = sum(1 for a in open_ if a["status"] == "merged")
        print("cc-scope: %d unsettled ask(s)%s%s. Each one is delivered by its OWN check passing "
              "(`cc-scope check-cmd %s <id> \"<cmd>\"` then `cc-scope deliver %s <id>`), or closed by hand when "
              "no command can measure it (`cc-scope verify %s <id> --evidence \"what you looked at\"`), or "
              "declined with a reason (`cc-scope decline %s <id> --why \"…\"`). A PR number is not any of "
              "those — leaving it is the one thing that is not a decision:"
              % (len(open_), where, " — %d of them merged but NOT delivered" % m if m else "",
                 repo, repo, repo, repo))
        for a in open_: print("  " + line(repo, a))
        return 1

    elif cmd in ("count", "digest"):
        rs = [args[0]] if args else repos()
        n = sum(len(select(r)) for r in rs)
        again = sum(1 for r in rs for a in select(r) if a.get("repeats", 1) > 1)
        if cmd == "count": print(n); return
        # "merged but not delivered" is its own number because it is the owner's own question — he asked
        # "has it landed yet / what is not done yet" three times, and a single open count could not answer it.
        m = sum(1 for r in rs for a in select(r) if a["status"] == "merged")
        why = ", ".join(filter(None, ["%d merged but not delivered" % m if m else "",
                                      "%d asked more than once" % again if again else ""]))
        if n: print("• 📋 %d ask%s of yours still open%s" % (n, "" if n == 1 else "s",
                                                            " (%s)" % why if why else ""))

    elif cmd == "missed":
        # Read out of the RECORD, not out of seen.log: these are the turns themselves, so anything listed
        # here can be promoted verbatim. Pending only — a turn somebody has already answered for is not a miss.
        everything, full = flag(args, "--all"), flag(args, "--full")
        since = float(opt(args, "--since", "24")) * 3600
        repo = args[0] if args else None
        rows = pending_turns(repo, 0 if everything else time.time() - since)
        pend, total, size = turns_size()
        if js:
            print(json.dumps({"pending": pend, "rows": total, "bytes": size, "turns": rows}, indent=2)); return
        when = "all of them" if everything else "last %gh" % (since / 3600)
        # The whole file, not this repo's slice: it is one record and its growth is one number.
        print("record: %d pending of %d rows, %s (%s)" % (pend, total, human(size), turns_path()))
        if not rows: print("nothing of his is waiting to be classified (%s)" % when); return
        print("owner turns kept, never classified (%s) — `promote <id>` or `dismiss <id> --why`:" % when)
        # A clipped list that does not say it is clipped is how this went wrong the first time.
        if len(rows) > 40: print("  … %d older ones not printed — --json has every one" % (len(rows) - 40))
        for r in rows[-40:]:
            print("  %s %s %s  %s" % (r["id"], r["ts"], r["repo"], r.get("src") or "-"))
            print("      %s" % (r["text"] if full else " ".join(r["text"].split())[:160]))

    elif cmd == "promote":
        # The whole point of keeping the turn: the row is opened from HIS text, dated when he sent it, with
        # the thread it came from. Nothing is retyped and nothing is paraphrased.
        r = get_turn(args[0])
        if r.get("disposed"):
            sys.exit("cc-scope: %s was already %s%s" % (r["id"], r["disposed"],
                                                        " (%s)" % (r.get("ask") or r.get("why")) if
                                                        (r.get("ask") or r.get("why")) else ""))
        chk = opt(args, "--check")
        refuse_doomed(r["repo"], "<new>", chk)
        aid, rep = add(r["repo"], r["text"], who=r.get("who") or "owner", src=r.get("src", ""),
                       track=opt(args, "--track"), done_when=opt(args, "--done-when"),
                       check=chk, at=r.get("ts", ""))
        dispose_turn(r["id"], "promoted", ask="%s %s" % (r["repo"], aid))
        print("%s %s%s ← %s" % (r["repo"], aid, " (asked again)" if rep else "", r["id"]))

    elif cmd == "dismiss":
        why = opt(args, "--why")
        if not why: sys.exit("cc-scope: dismiss needs --why — a turn leaves the list with a reason, like a decline")
        r = get_turn(args[0])
        if r.get("disposed"): sys.exit("cc-scope: %s was already %s" % (r["id"], r["disposed"]))
        dispose_turn(r["id"], "dismissed", why=why)
        print("%s dismissed: %s" % (r["id"], why))

    elif cmd == "classify":
        # The classifier is a filter that has been wrong and will be again, so this is how a session runs the
        # current one over what an older one skipped. Only the turns it now keeps move; the rest stay pending,
        # which is the honest answer rather than a second silent drop.
        tid = opt(args, "--id")
        rows = [get_turn(tid)] if tid else pending_turns(args[0] if args else None)
        rows = [r for r in rows if not r.get("disposed")]
        kept = 0
        for r in rows:
            if not is_ask(r["text"]): continue
            aid, _ = add(r["repo"], r["text"], who=r.get("who") or "owner", src=r.get("src", ""),
                         at=r.get("ts", ""))
            dispose_turn(r["id"], "classified", ask="%s %s" % (r["repo"], aid))
            print("  %s %s ← %s  %s" % (r["repo"], aid, r["id"], " ".join(r["text"].split())[:80]))
            kept += 1
        print("cc-scope: %d of %d pending turn(s) opened a row, %d still waiting" %
              (kept, len(rows), len(rows) - kept))

    elif cmd == "import":         # seed/replay: <at>TAB<src>TAB<track>TAB<status>TAB<evidence>TAB<text>
        repo, f = args[0], args[1]
        n = new = 0
        for raw in open(f):
            raw = raw.rstrip("\n")
            if not raw.strip() or raw.lstrip().startswith("#"): continue
            c = raw.split("\t")
            if len(c) < 6: sys.exit("cc-scope import: 6 tab-separated columns expected, got %d: %s" % (len(c), raw[:60]))
            at, src, track, status, ev, text = [x.strip() for x in c[:6]]
            aid, rep = add(repo, text, src=src, track=track, at=at, replay=True)
            n += 1; new += 0 if rep else 1
            if status and status != "open":
                if status not in STATES: sys.exit("cc-scope import: '%s' is not one of %s" % (status, "/".join(STATES)))
                edit(repo, aid, lambda a, s=status, e=ev: a.update(   # no `closed` on a backfill: when it was
                    status=s, **({"reason": e} if s == "declined" else {"evidence": e})))   # settled is not recorded
        print("cc-scope: imported %d ask(s) into %s (%d new)" % (n, repo, new))

    elif cmd == "hook":
        hook()

    elif cmd == "selfcheck":
        return selfcheck()

    else:
        sys.exit(__doc__)
    return 0


# ---- selfcheck -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
def selfcheck():
    global SCOPE, TURNS_KEEP, TURN_MAX
    import tempfile, shutil, io
    SCOPE = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-scope-selfcheck-")
    _fl = os.environ.get("CC_FAILURES")
    os.environ["CC_FAILURES"] = os.path.join(SCOPE, "failures")   # the deliver cases below write records: a scratch ledger, never the box's own
    def ledger(repo):
        r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-failures"), "show", "--json", "--scope", repo],
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
        return [x for x in (json.loads(r.stdout or "[]")) if x.get("kind") == "delivery/merged-not-delivered"]
    p = f = 0
    def ok(n): nonlocal p; p += 1; print("  ✓ %s" % n)
    def bad(n): nonlocal f; f += 1; print("  ✗ %s" % n)
    def t(n, cond): ok(n) if cond else bad(n)
    try:
        R = "_scopetest"
        t("an imperative is an ask", is_ask("add a scope ledger to the digest"))
        t("a standing rule is an ask", is_ask("from now on lead with the TLDR"))
        t("a complaint that names the fix is an ask", is_ask("the thread marks aren't working reliably, make them stick"))
        t("a bare thanks is not an ask", not is_ask("thanks!"))
        t("an ack with a few words is not an ask", not is_ask("ok cool, thanks a lot"))
        t("a ! command is not an ask", not is_ask("!status"))
        # A check runs with this file's own bin dir on PATH, so a row may name a sibling tool plainly (review of
        # the rc=127 landings, 2026-09-02): scrub PATH entirely and a bare sibling name still resolves.
        _sib = os.path.basename(os.path.realpath(__file__))
        _keep = os.environ.get("PATH", "")
        try:
            os.environ["PATH"] = ""
            t("a check finds this file's siblings on PATH even when PATH is empty",
              run_check(R, {"check": "command -v %s >/dev/null" % _sib})[0] == 0)
            t("and a name that is not a sibling still fails, so PATH is prepended and not replaced",
              run_check(R, {"check": "command -v definitely-not-a-tool-xyz >/dev/null"})[0] != 0)
        finally:
            os.environ["PATH"] = _keep
        t("a plain question is not opened as an ask", not is_ask("how full is the context?"))
        t("a compound ask keeps both parts",
          len(parts_of("react with :ok_hand: in #approvals. this should also remove it from the board.")) == 2)

        a1, rep = add(R, "make the digest show open asks", src="slack #demo 1788")
        t("add opens an ask", a1 == "a1" and not rep)
        a2, rep = add(R, "Make the digest   show open ASKS!", src="slack #demo 1799")
        t("the same ask twice is one row with repeats=2", a2 == a1 and rep and get(R, a1)["repeats"] == 2)
        t("check exits 1 while an ask is unsettled", quiet(["check", R]) == 1)
        quiet(["claim", R, a1, "--track", "digest"])
        t("a claimed ask is still work", get(R, a1)["status"] == "claimed" and quiet(["check", R]) == 1)

        # ---- MERGED IS NOT DELIVERED -------------------------------------------------------------------
        # The failure this whole section exists for: rows were closed on "merged as PR #N" while the daemon
        # still ran the old code, and the owner was the one who found out. A merge is now its own state, it
        # is not settled, and the only thing that moves a row past it is a command exiting 0.
        try:
            quiet(["merged", R, a1]); bad("merged without evidence is refused")
        except SystemExit as e: t("merged without evidence is refused", "evidence" in str(e))
        quiet(["merged", R, a1, "--evidence", "PR #64"])
        t("a MERGED ask is still work — a PR number is not delivery",
          get(R, a1)["status"] == "merged" and quiet(["check", R]) == 1)
        t("the line you read says so, in as many words",
          "NOT delivered" in run_capture(["list", R]) and "names no check" in run_capture(["list", R]))
        t("a merged ask with no check is listed as unverified and stays open",
          quiet(["unverified", R]) == 1 and "names no check" in run_capture(["unverified", R])
          and get(R, a1)["status"] == "merged")

        # a row with NO check cannot be delivered at all: exit 2, and it does not move
        t("deliver on a row that names no check is refused (exit 2) and the row does not move",
          quiet(["deliver", R, a1]) == 2 and get(R, a1)["status"] == "merged")

        # a check that FAILS cannot reach delivered, however many times it is run
        quiet(["check-cmd", R, a1, "exit 3"])
        t("a row whose check fails is NOT delivered and stays exactly where it was",
          quiet(["deliver", R, a1]) == 1 and get(R, a1)["status"] == "merged"
          and get(R, a1)["checked"]["rc"] == 3)
        t("the failure is written next to the ask, not swallowed",
          any("check FAILED" in n["msg"] for n in get(R, a1)["notes"]))
        # …AND TO THE FAILURES LEDGER, by this runner, once: "a merged fix still fails its check" is a failure of its own
        # (delivery/merged-not-delivered), keyed to the ask and the merge, so a second tick on the same red writes nothing.
        led = ledger(R)
        t("a merged ask whose check fails is recorded in the failures ledger as delivery/merged-not-delivered, by cc-scope",
          len(led) == 1 and led[0]["writer"] == "cc-scope" and led[0]["links"].get("ask") == a1
          and led[0]["links"]["source"].startswith("scope-deliver:%s:%s:" % (R, a1)) and "rc 3" in led[0]["observed"])
        quiet(["deliver", R, a1]); quiet(["deliver", R, a1])
        t("…once, not once per check tick: two more ticks on the same red leave one record", len(ledger(R)) == 1)
        R3 = R + "-ctl"   # its own ledger: an unsettled row left here would be work the cases below count
        a3, _ = add(R3, "a claimed ask whose check is red")
        quiet(["claim", R3, a3, "--track", "t3"]); quiet(["check-cmd", R3, a3, "exit 1"])
        t("control: a CLAIMED ask whose check fails is red but not merged, so it is not a merged-not-delivered record",
          quiet(["deliver", R3, a3]) == 1 and not ledger(R3))
        _ro = os.path.join(SCOPE, "ro"); os.makedirs(_ro); os.chmod(_ro, 0o500)
        _keepfl = os.environ["CC_FAILURES"]; os.environ["CC_FAILURES"] = os.path.join(_ro, "failures")
        try:
            _rc, err = run_capture_err(["deliver", R, a1])
            t("a writer failure (the ledger unwritable) leaves the verdict unchanged: still exit 1, still merged, one 'not recorded' line",
              _rc == 1 and get(R, a1)["status"] == "merged" and "failures ledger (%s): not recorded" % R in err)
        finally:
            os.chmod(_ro, 0o700); os.environ["CC_FAILURES"] = _keepfl
        try:
            quiet(["verify", R, a1, "--evidence", "I looked and it is fine, honestly"])
            bad("a red check cannot be talked round with a sentence")
        except SystemExit as e:
            t("a red check cannot be talked round with a sentence", "has not passed" in str(e))
        t("...and the row is still not settled", get(R, a1)["status"] == "merged" and quiet(["check", R]) == 1)

        # and a check that PASSES is what delivery means
        quiet(["check-cmd", R, a1, "true"])
        t("a check that passes is what makes a row delivered",
          quiet(["deliver", R, a1]) == 0 and get(R, a1)["status"] == "delivered")
        t("the evidence is the check itself, not a sentence about it",
          "check passed: true" in get(R, a1)["evidence"])
        t("a delivered ask is settled: it stops being work", quiet(["check", R]) == 0)
        t("a delivered ask is not unverified", quiet(["unverified", R]) == 0)
        t("re-running deliver is a no-op — same state, still delivered",
          quiet(["deliver", R, a1]) == 0 and get(R, a1)["status"] == "delivered")
        t("re-marking it merged does not drag a delivered row backwards",
          quiet(["merged", R, a1, "--evidence", "PR #64 again"]) == 0 and get(R, a1)["status"] == "delivered")

        # the by-hand path, for the ask no command can measure — it is a PERSON's word and it is marked as one
        a2b, _ = add(R, "make the tone of the updates warmer")
        t("an ask with no possible check still cannot be delivered", quiet(["deliver", R, a2b]) == 2)
        quiet(["verify", R, a2b, "--evidence", "read the last six updates in the channel myself"])
        t("a person may close what no command can measure", get(R, a2b)["status"] == "verified")
        t("...and it is still counted as never measured",
          quiet(["unverified", R]) == 1 and "closed by hand" in run_capture(["unverified", R]))

        # A row written before any of this existed sits in `delivered` on a sentence. Reading it as settled
        # now — same word, new meaning — would close it without anything ever having been measured, which is
        # this bug wearing the fix's clothes. It goes back on the queue.
        with Lock(R):
            d = load(R); d["asks"][a1] = dict(d["asks"][a1], status="delivered", checked={},
                                              evidence="merged as PR #64 on main; deployed"); save(R, d)
        t("a row closed on a sentence before checks existed is NOT settled by the word 'delivered'",
          quiet(["check", R]) == 1 and a1 in run_capture(["list", R]))
        t("it says why, on the line you read", "back on the queue" in run_capture(["list", R]))
        t("and it is reported unverified, with the sentence it was closed on",
          quiet(["unverified", R]) == 1 and "closed on a sentence" in run_capture(["unverified", R]))
        quiet(["check-cmd", R, a1, "true"]); quiet(["deliver", R, a1])
        t("giving it a check that passes is what settles it", quiet(["check", R]) == 0)

        a3, _ = add(R, "rewrite the whole box in rust")
        try:
            quiet(["verify", R, a3]); bad("verify without any evidence is refused")
        except SystemExit as e: t("verify without any evidence is refused", "evidence" in str(e))

        # A decline is a DECISION: it needs a reason, it leaves the queue, and it stays in the ledger.
        try:
            quiet(["decline", R, a3]); bad("decline without a reason is refused")
        except SystemExit as e: t("decline without a reason is refused", "why" in str(e))
        quiet(["decline", R, a3, "--why", "no reader would gain anything and the box would be down for a week"])
        t("a declined ask stops counting as work", quiet(["check", R]) == 0)
        t("a declined ask stays in the ledger with its reason",
          get(R, a3)["status"] == "declined" and "down for a week" in get(R, a3)["reason"])
        t("the reason is on the line you actually read",
          "down for a week" in run_capture(["list", R, "--status", "declined"]))
        t("a declined ask is not hidden by --all", a3 in run_capture(["list", R, "--all"]))

        t("a replay matches any status and changes nothing",
          add(R, "make the digest show open asks", replay=True) == (a1, True) and get(R, a1)["repeats"] == 2)
        t("a check is carried on the row from the moment it is opened",
          get(R, add(R, "keep the tmux server up", check="test -d /")[0])["check"] == "test -d /")
        quiet(["decline", R, a3, "--why", "still no"]) if get(R, a3)["status"] != "declined" else None
        a4, rep4 = add(R, "rewrite the whole box in rust")
        t("asked again after a decline, it is back on the queue with the reason kept",
          a4 == a3 and rep4 and get(R, a3)["status"] == "open" and "down for a week" in get(R, a3)["reason"])
        quiet(["decline", R, a3, "--why", "same answer as before"])
        a5, rep5 = add(R, "make the digest show open asks")
        t("asked again after it was delivered, it is a NEW row — that is a regression, not a repeat",
          a5 != a1 and not rep5)
        quiet(["decline", R, a5, "--why", "fixture"])

        add(R, "always run the gates before a PR", track="gates")
        t("check --track only measures that track's asks", quiet(["check", R, "--track", "nope"]) == 0)
        t("check --track sees its own", quiet(["check", R, "--track", "gates"]) == 1)

        # ---- A CHECK THAT CANNOT RUN IS NOT A CHECK THAT FAILED -----------------------------------------
        # a69's boundary check was a script in its track's state dir. The track finished, the dir went with
        # it, and the row read "merged, NOT delivered — its check has not passed" for five days while nobody
        # had measured anything at all. The two cases below are that pair; they must never read alike again.
        aC, _ = add(R, "prove the workspace boundary holds from inside")
        quiet(["merged", R, aC, "--evidence", "PR #156"])
        quiet(["check-cmd", R, aC, "exit 3"])
        t("a check that RAN and said no is a FAILURE: exit 1, written on the row as one",
          quiet(["deliver", R, aC]) == 1 and get(R, aC)["checked"]["rc"] == 3
          and any("check FAILED" in n["msg"] for n in get(R, aC)["notes"]))
        t("...and the line you read says the check has not passed",
          "its check has not passed" in run_capture(["list", R]))
        gone = os.path.join(HOME, ".cc", "scope", CHECKS, "_selfcheck-no-such-check.sh")
        quiet(["check-cmd", R, aC, "bash %s" % gone])
        t("a check that CANNOT RUN is a different answer: exit 3, not the 1 a real failure gets",
          quiet(["deliver", R, aC]) == 3 and get(R, aC)["checked"]["rc"] in CANNOT_RUN)
        t("...and the row says no answer, not failure",
          any("COULD NOT RUN" in n["msg"] for n in get(R, aC)["notes"])
          and not any("check FAILED (rc 127" in n["msg"] for n in get(R, aC)["notes"]))
        t("...and so does every line a person reads: list, unverified, show",
          "CANNOT RUN" in run_capture(["list", R])
          and "NEVER MEASURED" in run_capture(["unverified", R])
          and "COULD NOT RUN" in run_capture(["show", R, aC]))
        t("...and no answer still never delivers a row",
          get(R, aC)["status"] == "merged" and quiet(["check", R]) == 1)

        # ---- THE ADVICE MUST FIT THE REASON, NOT JUST THE CODE -------------------------------------------
        # CANNOT_RUN has two real causes and #297 only wrote advice for one: `gone` above is a script that
        # truly vanished, and pointing the reader at "give it a command that is there" is correct for it. But
        # a check can run PERFECTLY and choose rc 126/127 itself to say it could not measure something (the
        # rebuilt member boundary check, 2026-09-07) — the interpreter never failed to find it, so telling the
        # reader their command is missing sends them to fix a path that was never broken.
        rc, out = run_capture_err(["deliver", R, aC])   # aC's check is still `bash <gone>` from just above
        t("the vanished-script cause still gets told to give it a command that is there",
          rc == 3 and "give it a command that is there" in out.lower())
        quiet(["check-cmd", R, aC, "echo could not start 2 of 5, nothing failed >&2; exit 126"])
        rc2, out2 = run_capture_err(["deliver", R, aC])
        t("a check that RAN and chose CANNOT_RUN itself is still read as no answer, not a failure",
          rc2 == 3 and get(R, aC)["checked"]["rc"] == 126)
        t("...but it is NOT told its command is missing — that diagnosis does not fit what happened",
          "give it a command" not in out2.lower())
        t("...it is pointed at the check's own output and at re-running the SAME check",
          "read what it printed" in out2.lower() and "cc-scope deliver %s %s" % (R, aC) in out2)

        # The other half: a check must outlive the track that wrote it, and a check that goes missing must
        # not drag a row that already PASSED back onto the queue — that is this bug pointing the other way.
        src = os.path.join(SCOPE, "_a-track", "boundary-check.sh")
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(src), exist_ok=True)
        with open(src, "w") as fh: fh.write("exit 0\n")
        aD, _ = add(R, "prove the tab renders the block")
        quiet(["merged", R, aD, "--evidence", "PR #200"])
        quiet(["check-cmd", R, aD, "--script", src])
        kept = get(R, aD)["check"].split()[1]
        t("--script keeps the script beside the LEDGER, not in the track that wrote it",
          os.path.dirname(kept) == check_dir() and os.path.isfile(kept))
        t("and a row delivers from there", quiet(["deliver", R, aD]) == 0)
        shutil.rmtree(os.path.dirname(src))          # the track ends and the janitor takes its dir
        t("...and the check still runs after the track's own directory is gone",
          quiet(["deliver", R, aD]) == 0 and get(R, aD)["status"] == "delivered")
        os.remove(kept)                              # and now the check itself is unreachable
        t("a check that has gone missing does NOT un-deliver a row that already passed",
          quiet(["deliver", R, aD]) == 3 and get(R, aD)["status"] == "delivered" and passed(get(R, aD)))

        aE, _ = add(R, "prove the member boundary")
        doomed_cmd = "bash %s/.cc/state/%s/some-track/boundary-check.sh" % (HOME, R)
        try:
            quiet(["check-cmd", R, aE, doomed_cmd]); bad("a check run out of a track's state dir is refused")
        except SystemExit as e:
            t("a check run out of a track's state dir is refused, and says where to put it instead",
              "--script" in str(e))
        t("...but the SAME path merely looked at is not refused — 'the janitor took it' is a real check",
          quiet(["check-cmd", R, aE, "test ! -f %s/.cc/state/%s/some-track/boundary-check.sh" % (HOME, R)]) == 0)
        t("...and running a script that merely takes a temp path as an argument is not refused either",
          quiet(["check-cmd", R, aE, "bash install.sh /tmp/out.log"]) == 0)
        # …and `add --check` is the SAME door: it was open while check-cmd's was shut, which is a documented
        # way to write exactly the row this refusal exists to prevent (review of #297).
        try:
            quiet(["add", R, "another ask", "--check", doomed_cmd]); bad("add --check is refused too")
        except SystemExit as e:
            t("a doomed check given at `add --check` is refused the same way check-cmd's is", "--script" in str(e))
        # ASSERT THE CHECK LANDED, not that the call returned an int — the first spelling of this case was
        # `isinstance(quiet([...]), int)`, which quiet() satisfies unconditionally, and it is why a bug that
        # dropped --check on the floor shipped green (review of #297).
        fine = "test -f /etc/hostname"
        newid = run_capture(["add", R, "an ask with a fine check", "--check", fine]).split()[1]
        t("...and an ordinary check at `add --check` is still what ends up ON the row",
          get(R, newid).get("check") == fine)

        aid, _ = capture(R, '<channel source="cc-slack" chat_id="C1" thread_ts="17.1" ts="17.2" '
                            'user="ada.byron" role="owner" channel="#demo">\nplease put the open asks on Home',
                         src="")
        t("an owner Slack message is captured with its thread as the source",
          aid and get(R, aid)["src"].startswith("slack #demo 17.1"))
        t("a member's message is not an owner ask",
          capture(R, '<channel source="cc-slack" role="member" channel="#help" user="sam">fix the printer')[0] is None)
        t("a skipped message is still logged, so a miss can be found",
          "skip:not-owner" in open(os.path.join(SCOPE, SEEN)).read())

        # ---- A REACTION IS NOT AN ASK -----------------------------------------------------------------
        # The three that were really in a repo's ledger here, verbatim. Every cue word in them
        # is OURS — cc-slack quotes sixty characters of the message that was reacted to — and each one was
        # read and declined by hand. `is_ask(said)` is the half that matters: it holds each fixture to
        # carrying a directive, so these cases fail if they ever stop testing what they were written for.
        def reacted(said, kind="reaction"):
            return ('<channel source="cc-slack" chat_id="C1" thread_ts="17.1" ts="17.3" user="ada.byron" '
                    '%srole="owner" channel="#demo">\n%s' % ('kind="%s" ' % kind if kind else "", said))
        thumbs = {
            "our own 'Fix on its way'":
                '👍 on your 23:19 reply: "Agreed, that is the prompt\'s first line. Fix on its way: eac"',
            "our own 'rather than' in a name we proposed":
                '👍 on your 13:26 reply: "#demo-ask — it says what you do there rather than what answ"',
            "our own 'rather than' in a promise we made":
                '👍 on your 16:29 reply: "Will do — I\'ll lift it myself at cutover rather than asking "',
        }
        for whose, said in thumbs.items():
            t("a reaction is not an ask when the cue inside it is %s" % whose,
              is_ask(said) and capture(R, reacted(said))[0] is None)
        t("...and a 👎, or one taken off again, says just as little that belongs in a ledger of requests",
          capture(R, reacted('👎 on your 09:02 reply: "hold the deploy rather than landing it now"'))[0] is None
          and capture(R, reacted('👍 taken off your 09:02 reply: "hold the deploy rather than landing '
                                 'it now"'))[0] is None)
        t("...decided on the tag and never on the shape of the quote: the same words untagged still open a row",
          capture(R, reacted('👍 on your 11:00 reply: "we should rename it rather than minting a new one"',
                             kind=""))[0] is not None)
        t("...and the skip is logged like any other, so a wrong call is still findable",
          "skip:reaction" in open(os.path.join(SCOPE, SEEN)).read())
        # The three real ones were declined by hand. `add` puts a DECLINED row back on the queue when
        # the same text arrives again, so a reaction landing twice must not walk that path.
        already = thumbs["our own 'Fix on its way'"]
        aRx, _ = add(R, already)                      # the row exactly as the old capture path opened it
        quiet(["decline", R, aRx, "--why", "a 👍 on my own reply, captured as an ask"])
        t("a reaction already declined by hand is not re-opened when the same one arrives again",
          capture(R, reacted(already))[0] is None and get(R, aRx)["status"] == "declined")
        t("...and it is skipped through the hook too, while a real ask in that thread still records",
          hook_asks(reacted(thumbs["our own 'rather than' in a promise we made"])) == 0
          and hook_asks('<channel source="cc-slack" chat_id="C1" thread_ts="17.1" ts="17.4" user="ada.byron" '
                        'role="owner" channel="#demo">rename the channel rather than minting a second one') == 1)

        t("the hook is silent on a worker's own task prompt", capture_worker_silent())

        # ---- ONLY A PERSON OPENS A ROW ----------------------------------------------------------------
        # One case per shape in NOT_A_PERSON. `would_have` is the half that matters: each fixture carries a
        # real directive, so before the skip list every one of these opened a row that was declined by hand.
        def would_have(x): return is_ask(strip_tag(x)[0])
        def skipped(x): return would_have(x) and hook_asks(x) == 0
        t("a harness task-notification is not an ask",
          skipped("<task-notification>\n<task-id>b9j3x</task-id>\n"
                  "<summary>the digest is not working, you must restart it</summary>\n</task-notification>"))
        t("a peer session's cross-session-message is not an ask",
          skipped('<cross-session-message from="uds:/run/user/1000/cc-socks/505378.sock" from-name="dev-2b">'
                  "\nplease add the failing track to the board."))
        t("a SYSTEM NOTIFICATION is not an ask",
          skipped("[SYSTEM NOTIFICATION] the session is near its context limit and must be restarted before the next task"))
        t("a cross-session idle notice is not an ask",
          skipped('[Cross-session idle notice] "demo-01" is idle now; you should check what it left behind'))
        t("a slash-command block is not an ask",
          skipped("<command-name>/gates</command-name>\n<command-args>please run the gates and open the PR</command-args>"))
        t("a local command's own stdout is not an ask",
          skipped("<local-command-stdout>from now on the gates must be green before a PR</local-command-stdout>"))
        t("the board watchdog talking to itself through inject is not an owner ask",
          skipped('<channel source="cc-slack" chat_id="local" thread_ts="" ts="1788229590.5" user="local" '
                  'role="owner" channel="local" target="demo">board watchdog: demo/worker-sandbox — '
                  "it is waiting on you; ccbox refuses demo by design and runs claude with no "
                  "cc-guard inside. (set to waiting)"))
        t("a non-owner Slack message is not an ask, through the hook too",
          skipped('<channel source="cc-slack" chat_id="C9" ts="18.1" user="sam" role="member" '
                  'channel="#help">please fix the printer'))
        # cc-handoff types a prompt into a session at both ends of a handoff — the successor's opening prompt
        # and the retiring session's stop message — and each opened a row that session then declined by hand
        # (a121, a126, a131; a132). It leads every prompt it types with `[cc-handoff]` (its MACHINE), so what
        # is matched here is the tag: the first two fixtures are shaped like today's prompts, the third is a
        # sentence this file has never seen, and all three are skipped the same way.
        t("the prompt that starts a successor session is not an owner ask",
          skipped("[cc-handoff] 9f2c1a7b04d3: you are the SUCCESSOR for myrepo/x — your brief is in your "
                  "system prompt. Start now: run `cc-handoff --brief myrepo/x` first, and ask the "
                  "predecessor what the packet did not answer."))
        t("the stop message the retiring session gets is not either",
          skipped("[cc-handoff] Handoff complete: your successor is live and owns this target now. Append "
                  "your final entry to the journal — what is done, what is next, gotchas — and stop. You "
                  "are no longer the live session: anything typed here is relayed to your successor."))
        t("...nor is a prompt cc-handoff types later, in wording this file has never been taught",
          skipped("[cc-handoff] your predecessor left the branch unpushed. Push it before you journal."))
        # The harness's plain-sentence notices — no wrapper, and each one carries a real instruction, which
        # is why the reset notice opened a row in four repos' ledgers on 2026-09-04. One case per notice.
        t("the usage-limit reset notice is not an ask",
          skipped("Your claude.ai usage limit has reset. Continue the task you were working on when the "
                  "limit was reached; do not repeat work that is already complete."))
        t("nor is the limit being hit, however the harness words it",
          skipped("Claude usage limit reached. Your limit will reset at 3pm. Continue the task you were "
                  "on then; do not repeat work that is already complete."))
        t("nor a model reroute banner",
          skipped("Opus limit reached · now using Sonnet 4. Keep going with the task you were given; "
                  "do not start over."))
        t("nor the banner a resumed session opens with",
          skipped("This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. "
                  "The summary below covers the earlier portion. Please continue the conversation from "
                  "where it left off without asking the user any further questions."))
        # ...and the four things the filter must never eat.
        t("an owner ask ABOUT the usage limit still records",
          hook_asks("the usage limit keeps eating the queue — make cc-loop wait for the reset instead "
                    "of dying") == 1)
        t("an owner ask that merely starts with the word cc-handoff still records",
          hook_asks("cc-handoff leaves the old window alive after a retire — please fix that before the "
                    "next one") == 1)
        t("an owner prompt typed in the terminal still records",
          hook_asks("add the skip list to the ask-capture hook") == 1)
        t("an owner Slack message still records",
          hook_asks('<channel source="cc-slack" chat_id="C1" thread_ts="17.1" ts="17.2" user="ada.byron" '
                    'role="owner" channel="#demo">put the open asks on Home, please') == 1)
        # ---- THE RETIRING SIDE OF A HANDOFF: cc-handoff --relay decides (payload over stdin), the hook hands it back
        retired = {"id": "fixture", "target": "_hooktest", "live": "successor", "phase": "retired",
                   "predecessor": {"session_id": "sid-retired", "tmux": "", "window": "_hooktest~old"},
                   "successor": {"session_id": "sid-live", "tmux": "", "window": "_hooktest"}}
        n, out = hook_run("please add the relay case to the hook", sid="sid-retired", record=retired)
        t("a prompt into a RETIRED session comes back blocked with the pointer, and opens no row",
          n == 0 and '"block"' in out and "use window _hooktest" in out)
        t("...and from any other session the same prompt is neither blocked nor lost",
          hook_run("please add the relay case to the hook", record=retired) == (1, ""))
        # ---- KEPT BEFORE IT IS CLASSIFIED --------------------------------------------------------------
        # The real miss, verbatim (2026-09-08 01:0xZ). One sentence, opening with an ack, with every
        # cue it carries sitting behind that ack — so it was skipped, and the only copy of it left was a
        # seen.log line clipped at 160 characters.
        miss = "Ok well do whatever you need to speed up the whole landing and review process"
        t("a directive that opens with an acknowledgement is an ask", is_ask(miss))
        t("...and what is kept is his whole sentence, ack and all", parts_of(miss) == [miss])
        t("...while a bare ack, and an ack that only compliments, are still not asks",
          not is_ask("thanks!") and not is_ask("ok cool, thanks a lot")
          and not is_ask("nice, this is exactly what I was hoping for, really good work"))

        # `rec` is how every case below reads the record back, and it answers {} rather than raising: a
        # missing record is the failure these cases exist to report, and one that ends the run reports
        # nothing about the cases after it.
        def rec(txt):
            r = [x for x in read_turns() if x["text"] == txt]
            return r[0] if len(r) == 1 else {}

        aM, _ = capture(R, '<channel source="cc-slack" chat_id="C1" thread_ts="19.1" ts="19.2" '
                           'user="ada.byron" role="owner" channel="#demo">\n' + miss)
        rM = rec(miss)
        t("that exact message is kept in full, with its thread, and opens a row",
          aM and rM and rM["src"].startswith("slack #demo 19.1")
          and rM["disposed"] == "classified" and get(R, aM)["text"] == miss)

        # ...and one the classifier skips is kept just the same, and stays PENDING until somebody answers for it
        skipped_turn = "ok, ship it"
        capture(R, skipped_turn, src="terminal ab12cd34")
        pend = rec(skipped_turn)
        t("a turn the classifier skips opens no row and is kept anyway",
          not is_ask(skipped_turn) and pend and not pend["disposed"])
        t("...and it is what `missed` lists, by an id you can act on",
          pend and pend["id"] in run_capture(["missed", R, "--all"]))
        before = len(load(R)["asks"])
        if pend: quiet(["promote", pend["id"]])
        prom = [a for a in load(R)["asks"].values() if a["text"] == skipped_turn]
        t("promote opens the ask from the kept text — his words, his source, the time he sent it",
          len(load(R)["asks"]) == before + 1 and len(prom) == 1 and pend
          and prom[0]["src"] == "terminal ab12cd34" and prom[0]["asked"] == pend["ts"])
        t("...and the record is disposed of, so it drops off the missed list",
          pend and get_turn(pend["id"])["disposed"] == "promoted"
          and pend["id"] not in run_capture(["missed", R, "--all"]))

        capture(R, "ok nice one", src="terminal ab12cd34")
        dmr = rec("ok nice one")
        dm = dmr.get("id", "nothing-was-kept")
        try:
            quiet(["dismiss", dm]); bad("dismiss without a reason is refused")
        except SystemExit as e: t("dismiss without a reason is refused", "--why" in str(e))
        t("a dismissed turn leaves the list with the reason on the record",
          bool(dmr) and quiet(["dismiss", dm, "--why", "an ack on its own, nothing asked"]) == 0
          and get_turn(dm)["disposed"] == "dismissed" and dm not in run_capture(["missed", R, "--all"]))
        # A turn an OLDER classifier skipped: `classify` is how a session runs today's over the backlog.
        tC = keep_turn(R, "please put the ask count on the tab", "ada.byron", "terminal old")
        t("classify runs the classifier again and opens the rows it now keeps",
          quiet(["classify", R]) == 0 and get_turn(tC)["disposed"] == "classified"
          and any(a["text"] == "please put the ask count on the tab" for a in load(R)["asks"].values()))

        # ---- A TOKEN HE PASTED IS NOT KEPT ------------------------------------------------------------
        # split so the file itself never holds a token shape: GitHub push protection refused the public publish on it
        tok = "xox" + "b-2222222222-3333333333-abcdefghijklmnop"
        capture(R, "the deploy token is %s by the way" % tok, src="terminal ab12cd34")
        rT = [r for r in read_turns() if r["text"].startswith("the deploy token is")]
        t("a token in a turn is redacted before the turn is written down",
          len(rT) == 1 and tok not in rT[0]["text"] and "[redacted]" in rT[0]["text"])
        if rT: quiet(["promote", rT[0]["id"]])
        aT = [a for a in load(R)["asks"].values() if a["text"].startswith("the deploy token is")]
        t("...and the ask promoted from it carries the redacted text, not the token",
          len(aT) == 1 and tok not in aT[0]["text"] and "[redacted]" in aT[0]["text"])
        t("...and the token is nowhere in the record or the log beside it",
          not any(tok in json.dumps(r) for r in read_turns())
          and tok not in open(os.path.join(SCOPE, SEEN)).read())
        # THE COPY MUST NOT DRIFT. cc-slack owns this pattern; its source is parsed (never imported — that
        # would cost every prompt a parse of the whole file) and this copy is held to it character for character.
        sib = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-slack")
        theirs = ""
        try:
            import ast
            for nd in ast.walk(ast.parse(io.open(sib, encoding="utf-8").read())):
                if (isinstance(nd, ast.Assign) and getattr(nd.targets[0], "id", "") == "SECRET_RE"
                        and isinstance(nd.value, ast.Call) and nd.value.args):
                    theirs = ast.literal_eval(nd.value.args[0])
        except Exception: theirs = ""
        t("the secret pattern is cc-slack's own, not a copy that has drifted away from it",
          SECRET_RE.pattern == theirs if theirs else not os.path.exists(sib))

        keep_max, TURN_MAX = TURN_MAX, 200
        try:
            tB = keep_turn(R, "ok, " + "x" * 400, "ada.byron", "terminal ab12cd34")
            t("one absurd paste is cut, and the record says how much of it was not kept",
              len(get_turn(tB)["text"]) < 400 and "204 characters not kept" in get_turn(tB)["text"])
        finally:
            TURN_MAX = keep_max

        # ---- COMPACTION ARCHIVES DISPOSED RECORDS, IT DOES NOT TRIM BY AGE -----------------------------
        # seen.log keeps its last 400 lines, so a real ask aged out of the only copy of it. The way out of
        # this file is being disposed of, and even then the record is appended to the archive, not dropped.
        keep_n, TURNS_KEEP = TURNS_KEEP, 6
        try:
            stays = keep_turn(R, "ok whatever you think is best, your call", "ada.byron", "terminal ab12cd34")
            gone = [keep_turn(R, "filler turn %d" % i, "ada.byron", "terminal ab12cd34") for i in range(10)]
            for g in gone: dispose_turn(g, "dismissed", why="filler")
            keep_turn(R, "one more, which puts the file over the threshold", "ada.byron", "terminal ab12cd34")
            live = {r["id"] for r in read_turns()}
            t("a pending turn survives compaction however big the file gets",
              stays in live and get_turn(stays)["text"].startswith("ok whatever"))
            t("...and the disposed ones are archived, not deleted",
              not (set(gone) & live) and set(gone) <= {r["id"] for r in read_turns(TURNS_ARCHIVE)})
        finally:
            TURNS_KEEP = keep_n

        out = run_capture(["missed", R, "--all"])
        t("`missed` says how big the record has got — pending, rows and bytes, on one line",
          re.search(r"record: \d+ pending of \d+ rows, [\d.]+ ?\w+ \(", out))
        # .get(), not [], all the way through: a json form that lost the numbers is what this case is for,
        # and it should say so rather than end the run on a KeyError.
        jm = json.loads(run_capture(["missed", R, "--all", "--json"]))
        t("...and the json form carries the same three numbers beside the turns",
          isinstance(jm, dict) and jm.get("rows") == len(read_turns())
          and jm.get("pending") == len(pending_turns())
          and jm.get("bytes") == os.path.getsize(turns_path())
          and [x["id"] for x in jm.get("turns", [])] == [x["id"] for x in pending_turns(R)])

        # A MACHINE'S TURN IS NOT KEPT — decided on provenance, never on how the sentence reads.
        was = len(read_turns())
        t("a machine's turn is not kept at all, though a real one right after it is",
          hook_asks("Your claude.ai usage limit has reset. Continue the task you were working on when the "
                    "limit was reached.") == 0
          and hook_asks("<task-notification>\n<summary>the digest is down, restart it</summary>\n"
                        "</task-notification>") == 0
          and len(read_turns()) == was
          and hook_asks("ok well do whatever you need to speed the landing up") == 1
          and len(read_turns()) == was + 1)

        n = int(run_capture(["count", R]))
        t("count is the number of unsettled asks", n == len(select(R)))
        t("digest says nothing when nothing is open", run_capture(["digest", "_empty"]).strip() == "")
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(SCOPE, ignore_errors=True)
        if _fl is None: os.environ.pop("CC_FAILURES", None)
        else: os.environ["CC_FAILURES"] = _fl
    print("== cc-scope selfcheck: %d passed, %d failed ==" % (p, f))
    return 1 if f else 0


def quiet(argv):
    """main() with its output swallowed — the selfcheck asserts on exit codes, not on the printed report.
    stderr too: the refusals below are meant to be long and explain themselves, and a passing suite should
    not scroll a page of advice for a case that behaved exactly as it was asserted to."""
    import io, contextlib
    buf = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf), contextlib.redirect_stderr(buf): return main(argv) or 0


def run_capture(argv):
    import io, contextlib
    buf = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf): main(argv)
    return buf.getvalue()


def run_capture_err(argv):
    """(exit code, stdout+stderr) — `deliver`'s CANNOT_RUN advice is written to stderr, and telling one cause
    of it from another means asserting on the words, not just on the row's state afterwards."""
    import io, contextlib
    buf = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf), contextlib.redirect_stderr(buf): rc = main(argv) or 0
    return rc, buf.getvalue()


def hook_asks(text):
    return hook_run(text)[0]


def hook_run(text, sid="selfcheck", record=None):
    """Run the UserPromptSubmit hook over one prompt: (rows the ledger gained, what it printed). The hook's
    other two guards are neutralised: the selfcheck usually runs inside a worker's worktree, which would
    pass every skip case for entirely the wrong reason and fail the two that must still record. Hermetic:
    `cc-handoff --relay` reads CC_HANDOFF_DIR, and here that is the temp dir — `record` (a handoff record) or
    nothing — never the box's own."""
    import tempfile, shutil, io, contextlib
    d = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-scope-hooktest-")       # under /tmp: no .cc/track marker, no repo name
    keep, old = {k: os.environ.get(k) for k in ("CC_ROLE", "CC_REPO", "CC_HANDOFF_DIR")}, sys.stdin
    os.environ.pop("CC_ROLE", None); os.environ["CC_REPO"] = "_hooktest"; os.environ["CC_HANDOFF_DIR"] = d
    if record: json.dump(record, open(os.path.join(d, "fixture.json"), "w"))
    try:
        before = len(load("_hooktest")["asks"])
        sys.stdin = io.StringIO(json.dumps({"prompt": text, "cwd": d, "session_id": sid}))
        buf = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf): hook()
        return len(load("_hooktest")["asks"]) - before, buf.getvalue()
    finally:
        sys.stdin = old
        for k, v in keep.items(): os.environ.pop(k, None) if v is None else os.environ.update({k: v})
        shutil.rmtree(d, ignore_errors=True)


def capture_worker_silent():
    """The hook must ignore a track worktree — cc dispatches workers by writing their task into the prompt.
    Hermetic like hook_run: CC_HANDOFF_DIR is this temp dir, never the box's own. On 2026-09-04 a landing's gate
    ran this case from a retiring session's shell; `cc-handoff --relay` matched the box's real record by
    $TMUX_PANE and typed the fixture prompt into the live successor, twice, where it read as the owner's ask."""
    import tempfile, shutil, io, contextlib
    d = tempfile.mkdtemp()
    keep = os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF_DIR"); os.environ["CC_HANDOFF_DIR"] = d
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(d, ".cc")); open(os.path.join(d, ".cc", "track"), "w").write("r\nt\n")
        payload = json.dumps({"prompt": "build the ledger and open a PR", "cwd": d, "session_id": "x"})
        old = sys.stdin; sys.stdin = io.StringIO(payload)
        buf = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf): hook()
        sys.stdin = old
        return buf.getvalue() == "" and not os.path.exists(path(repo_of(d) or "box"))
    finally:
        os.environ.pop("CC_HANDOFF_DIR", None) if keep is None else os.environ.update({"CC_HANDOFF_DIR": keep})
        shutil.rmtree(d, ignore_errors=True)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try: sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]) or 0)
    except IndexError: sys.exit(__doc__)
    except BrokenPipeError: sys.exit(0)
